Chapter: CapProcessFamily

 `[Process]` is the effect row guarding `get-args`, `get-env`,
 `current-dir`, `run-process`, `run-process-full` and `process-exit`, and
 until now no Codex program could declare it. `Process` was absent from
 capability-vocabulary, so `opening : [Process] ...` was refused with
 CDX4001, "no capability the manifest can carry" -- and the refusal is in
 the type checker rather than in manifest emission, so it fired on every
 target, the transpilers and IR included. The entire family resolved,
 type-checked, and could not be called by anything.

 It is a carriable capability now: one bit, one cap id, the same shape as
 Gpu or Identity. Its operations stay host-only and unimplemented on bare
 metal, which is the `Camera` arrangement and not a new
 kind of half-measure.

 This test asks the manifest question of it: does the capability
 survive the manifest and reach the granted process word, or is it known
 in one table and lost in another? Process declares no direction, so it
 is a single bit, 26. The bit is read through process-get-cap -- the
 granted word the kernel itself tests before a syscall, not a restatement
 of the source. A 1 means the name agreed all the way from
 capability-vocabulary through manifest-cap-id to boot-cap-mask-for.

 The test can fail, which is the point of picking this instrument. It
 reports the bit rather than asserting the program compiled: a program
 that merely compiles proves only that CDX4001 stopped firing, and would
 read exactly the same if the bit were dropped between the row and the
 grant.

Section: Bits

  cap-bit : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  cap-bit (word) (n) = if bit-and word (bit-shl 1 n) /= 0 then 1 else 0

Section: Entry

  opening : [Console, Process] Nothing
  opening = act
    print-line-uni ("process " & show (cap-bit (process-get-cap process-get-pid) 26))
  end
